Data Provenance and Citation Clarification

Issue Raised by AAS Reviewer

The reviewer noted that the origin of the simulation data used in this work must be clearly specified. 
While three prior studies are cited, none appear to provide publicly accessible simulation snapshots. 
If the analysis relies on snapshots from an existing simulation, this dependency should be stated explicitly, along with a concise description of the simulation setup and methodology. 
Alternatively, if the simulations were newly generated for this study following the methods described in the cited works, this should be clearly indicated and accompanied by a more detailed description of the simulation procedure than is currently provided.

Action Taken: Correspondence with Dr. Besla

I contacted Dr. Besla to request clarification regarding the use and citation of the Milky Way–Andromeda (MW–M31) merger simulation snapshots originally provided for the ASTR 400B course. 
I explained that these snapshots are being used to demonstrate and generate plots for an analysis pipeline that I have since substantially extended, with the intent of publishing the methodology as a research note for the American Astronomical Society (AAS). 
Given that the simulation data set does not appear to be publicly available, I requested guidance on whether its use is permitted for this purpose and how it should be properly cited in a publication.
